{
 id:"LP3-07", title:"Seminar Preparation: The Ethics of Facial Recognition", category:"Technology", band:7.5, mock:null, part:3,
 voices:{T:"sc_f", M:"gb_m", A:"us_f"},
 lines:[
 ["T","Come in, both of you, and grab a seat. So, your seminar on the ethics of facial recognition is a week on Friday. Where have you got to?"],
 ["M","Well, we've done a lot of reading, but we still haven't settled on a focus. I'd like to concentrate on police use in public spaces, because that's where the most serious questions about civil liberties come up."],
 ["A","The trouble is, three other groups are already covering policing. I think we'd have something fresher to say if we looked at shops and shopping centres, where retailers use cameras to pick out suspected shoplifters."],
 ["T","I'd agree with Aisha there. Private use gets far less attention, and the rules governing it are considerably weaker, so there's more to debate."],
 ["M","Fair enough. Retail it is, then."],
 ["T","Good. Now, how are you planning to open the session?"],
 ["M","I thought we could start with some statistics on how accurate the systems are, just to set the scene."],
 ["A","Numbers right at the start can feel a bit dry, though. What about describing a real case, the woman who was banned from every branch of a supermarket chain after a false match? It puts a human face on the issue."],
 ["T","Hmm, I'd be wary of that one, actually. The details of that case are still being disputed in court, so you'd risk presenting allegations as established fact."],
 ["A","Oh, I hadn't realised that."],
 ["T","Why not ask the group to imagine they've just been stopped at a shop entrance and told they're on a watch list? A hypothetical scenario like that raises exactly the same issues without the legal complications."],
 ["M","That could work. People will have a gut reaction straight away, and we can build the discussion on it."],
 ["A","OK, we'll go with the imaginary scenario."],
 ["T","And what would you say is the central ethical issue you want them to wrestle with?"],
 ["M","For me it's accuracy. The error rates are noticeably higher for some groups of people than for others, and that's a serious fairness problem."],
 ["A","But accuracy improves every year. If the software became perfect tomorrow, would the problem disappear? I don't think so. What bothers me is that shoppers never actually agree to having their faces scanned. There's usually just a tiny sign by the door."],
 ["T","I think that's the more productive argument for a seminar, because it doesn't depend on the technology failing. Make consent the core of the session, and treat accuracy as a secondary point."],
 ["M","Alright, I can live with that, as long as the accuracy figures still get a mention."],
 ["T","Of course. Let's look at your sources, then. Which ones are you relying on?"],
 ["M","The main one so far is the Halvorsen report on biometric surveillance. It's very comprehensive."],
 ["T","It is thorough, but bear in mind it came out in twenty sixteen. The systems have changed almost beyond recognition since then, so I wouldn't lean on it too heavily."],
 ["A","Then there's the Linwood University study, which tested five commercial systems in real shops."],
 ["M","I found that one quite hard going, to be honest. Pages and pages of algorithms."],
 ["A","Yes, but the figures in it are exactly what we need. As far as I can tell, it's the only source with trustworthy error rates for retail settings."],
 ["T","Agreed. Use it for the numbers and skip the mathematical sections."],
 ["M","We also watched a documentary called The Watched Aisle. It has some really moving interviews with shoppers who'd been wrongly stopped."],
 ["A","It's powerful, but it never gives the retailers a chance to explain themselves. It's completely one-sided."],
 ["M","And finally there's the guidance published by the Retail Security Association."],
 ["T","Just remember that the association is funded by the companies that sell these systems, so they have every reason to present them favourably. Read it critically."],
 ["A","Noted. Is there anything else we should think about for the format?"],
 ["T","Yes. For the discussion, divide the group into small teams and give each team a role to argue from, say a shop owner, a shopper or a regulator. It stops people simply giving their own opinions."],
 ["M","And how long should our part be?"],
 ["T","Keep your own presentation to around ten minutes. Some groups talk for half an hour, but a seminar should be mostly discussion."],
 ["A","That's a relief, actually."],
 ["T","Finally, send me a one-page outline by Wednesday, not Thursday as I said in the email, so I can circulate it to everyone in advance."],
 ["M","Wednesday. Got it. Thanks, that's really helpful."]
 ],
 groups:[
 {qtype:"Multiple Choice", instr:"Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.", items:[
  {q:"The students agree that the seminar will focus on the use of facial recognition by", opts:["the police in public spaces.","retailers in shops.","shopping centre managers to count visitors."], a:"B", tag:"distractors", ev:"Fair enough. Retail it is, then.", ex:"Marcus suggests policing, but Aisha rejects it because three other groups are covering it, and the tutor supports her. Shopping centres are mentioned only as places where retailers use cameras, not for counting visitors.", strat:"In Part 3, an idea is only the answer once the other speakers stop objecting to it."},
  {q:"How will the students begin the seminar?", opts:["by presenting accuracy statistics","by describing a recent court case","by asking the group to imagine a situation"], a:"C", tag:"distractors", ev:"OK, we'll go with the imaginary scenario.", ex:"Statistics are 'a bit dry' (Aisha) and the real case is 'still being disputed in court' (tutor). The tutor's 'hypothetical scenario' = imagine a situation."},
  {q:"What does the tutor say should be the main ethical issue in the discussion?", opts:["the lack of consent from shoppers","unequal error rates between groups","the cost of the technology for small shops"], a:"A", tag:"paraphrasing", ev:"Make consent the core of the session, and treat accuracy as a secondary point.", ex:"'Shoppers never actually agree' = lack of consent. Marcus's point about error rates (B) becomes only 'a secondary point'. Cost is never discussed."}
 ]},
 {qtype:"Matching", instr:"What comment is made about each source? Choose FOUR answers from the box and write the correct letter, A-F, next to each source.", options:["A  It is out of date.","B  It is too difficult to understand.","C  It provides reliable data.","D  It presents only one point of view.","E  It comes from an organisation with a financial interest.","F  It is the most comprehensive source."], optKey:"letter", items:[
  {q:"the Halvorsen report", a:"A", tag:"paraphrasing", ev:"bear in mind it came out in twenty sixteen. The systems have changed almost beyond recognition since then", ex:"Marcus calls it 'very comprehensive' (F), but the tutor's key comment is that it is from 2016 and the technology has changed, i.e. out of date."},
  {q:"the Linwood University study", a:"C", tag:"distractors", ev:"it's the only source with trustworthy error rates for retail settings", ex:"Marcus finds it 'hard going' (B), but Aisha and the tutor agree to use it for its numbers: 'trustworthy error rates' = reliable data."},
  {q:"The Watched Aisle documentary", a:"D", tag:"paraphrasing", ev:"It's completely one-sided.", ex:"It 'never gives the retailers a chance to explain themselves'. The moving interviews are praised, but no option refers to them."},
  {q:"the Retail Security Association guidance", a:"E", tag:"detail", ev:"the association is funded by the companies that sell these systems", ex:"Funding from the sellers of the systems = a financial interest, so the students must read it critically."}
 ]},
 {qtype:"Sentence Completion", instr:"Complete the sentences below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.", limit:2, items:[
  {q:"Each small team in the discussion will be given a ___ to argue from.", a:["role"], tag:"detail", ev:"give each team a role to argue from", ex:"For example a shop owner, a shopper or a regulator, so that people do not just give their own opinions."},
  {q:"The students' own presentation should last about ___.", a:["10 minutes","ten minutes"], tag:"numbers", ev:"Keep your own presentation to around ten minutes.", ex:"'Around' = about. Half an hour is what some other groups do, which the tutor criticises."},
  {q:"The tutor needs a one-page outline by ___.", a:["Wednesday"], tag:"distractors", ev:"send me a one-page outline by Wednesday, not Thursday as I said in the email", ex:"The tutor corrects the earlier deadline: Thursday was in the email, but the new deadline is Wednesday."}
 ]}
 ]
}


